WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.020
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.700
In the previous
lecture, we have seen

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.780
how the gauge bosons, the W,
and the Z boson acquire mass,

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.300
while the photon
remains massless,

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.100
through the Higgs mechanism.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:25.620
We introduced a new field,
a new complex doublet field,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:29.100
the Higgs field,
which then broke,

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.460
through its vacuum expectation
value, the symmetry.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.340
And then through the
coupling to the gauge bosons,

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.470
they acquired mass.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.630
Right.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.210
But we also have to find a
solution for the fermions.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:45.960
You cannot simply add a fermion
mass onto the Lagrangian.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.930
That would change, or
that could violate, break,

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:51.270
the gauge invariant.

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:52.540
So how do we do this?

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.630
We do this in a
very similar way--

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:55.650
even easier.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.660
But before we look
into how this is done,

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:00.285
let's have a look at
the masses itself.

00:01:00.285 --> 00:01:02.640
It's spectacular.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.330
The top quark is our
heaviest known fermion.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:11.030
It has a mass of about 172 GeV.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:15.230
The tau has a mass of 1.7 GeV.

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:17.360
The muon is an
order of magnitude

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.850
lighter, with 0.1 GeV.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:29.750
And for the electron, we
have to go to 0.51 MeV.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:30.860
OK?

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.500
And we haven't even
tried to understand,

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.450
or we were not able
to measure, actually,

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:36.800
the masses of neutrinos.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.500
We will talk about neutrinos in
one of the following lectures.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.950
So here you have six
orders of magnitude.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:47.060
And you have to go
further down here in order

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:51.510
to find the neutrinos
on this mass scale.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.380
So we have to have a
mass-giving mechanism which

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:59.600
allows this broad spectrum
of masses to occur.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.150
And the very simple
ad hoc mechanism

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.310
which was introduced
to the standard model

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:09.110
is one where the particle simply
interacts with the Higgs field.

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:11.810
So we have our Higgs field here.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.660
Let's say we have a
left-handed particle coming in.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:22.740
And the interaction
with the Higgs field

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.230
turns it into a
right-handed particle.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.900
This is a little bit
simplified, but what

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.690
we do there here is
simply introducing terms

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.940
into the Lagrangian
which do nothing else.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:34.802
We turn our
left-handed particles,

00:02:34.802 --> 00:02:36.510
via the interaction
with the Higgs field,

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:38.680
into right-handed, and
the other way around.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.800
And we have to do this
for up-type particles

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.330
and for down-type particles.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:45.600
So here is another view of this.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:53.150
The strength here is the mass
of the particle over the vacuum

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:55.800
expectation value.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.440
This number here, this
number, this lambda d,

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.190
it's the so-called
Yukawa coupling.

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:06.150
And those Yukawa couplings now
change from fermion to fermion.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.950
Each fermion comes with
their own Yukawa coupling.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:12.030
It's basically a free
parameter in our theory

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:13.658
in the standard model.

00:03:13.658 --> 00:03:16.200
So instead of talking about the
masses being free parameters,

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.075
we talk about the coupling
to the Higgs field

00:03:18.075 --> 00:03:18.960
as free parameter.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:22.520
But they are one and the same.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:23.020
All right.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.550
So this was rather
straightforward.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.490
It's a simple coupling; you
introduce this term ad hoc,

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:29.500
and then hope for the
best that it's actually

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.640
realized in nature.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.500
And you'll see
that this is indeed

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.910
the case for some of
the fermions later.

